  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "QWeTNDJLtt0EnVqqTc2XMYeTxOU=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:54:15.722Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The state schema is GraphQL SDL, and the one piece of syntax that carries real semantics for a document model is the double `!` on a collection:\n\n```graphql\ntype TodoListState {\n  items: [TodoItem!]!\n}\n\ntype TodoItem {\n  id: OID!\n  text: String!\n  checked: Boolean!\n}\n```\n\n- the **inner** `!` on `TodoItem!` means no entry inside the list may be null;\n- the **outer** `!` on `[TodoItem!]!` means the list itself may not be null \u2014 \"it can be an empty list `[]`, but it cannot be absent\".\n\nThat is why the vault's own document-modelling rule holds that collections should always be `[Type!]!`: the outer `!` is what lets a document be created empty without the field being `null`, and the inner `!` is what stops a hole appearing inside it.\n\nThe other Powerhouse-specific piece is the **`OID`** scalar \u2014 a custom scalar for unique identifiers in document models, with automatic ID generation when used with `generateId()` from the document-model core library. Its purpose in the example is stated plainly: `id: OID!` is \"crucial for referencing specific items, for example when updating or deleting them\" \u2014 an operation input has to name the row it changes.\n\nBeyond the built-in scalars (`String`, `Int`, `Float`, `Boolean`, `ID`), the Document-Engineering system adds custom scalars **linked to reusable front-end components**, which is what makes a scalar choice a UI decision as well as a data one.\n\n## Provenance\n- `apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/02-DocumentModelCreation/02-SpecifyTheStateSchema.md` \u2014 Core concepts \u203a Scalars, Lists and non-null; Example: TodoList state schema \u203a Breakdown\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:54:15.722Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:54:15.722Z"
    }
  },
